WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.480
Cassandra is a database program that spreads its data over many equal computers.

2
00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.287
No computer is in charge.

3
00:00:10.533 --> 00:00:15.306
That sounds odd, because most offices and most programs have a boss somewhere.

4
00:00:15.306 --> 00:00:19.528
A boss is simple, right up to the day the boss is the one that fails.

5
00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:22.976
So how does every machine hear that something changed?

6
00:00:22.976 --> 00:00:25.888
Picture an office with six clerks and no manager.

7
00:00:25.888 --> 00:00:28.384
Each clerk keeps a copy of the staff list.

8
00:00:28.633 --> 00:00:33.666
A new clerk arrives in the morning, and by lunch every list has her name on it.

9
00:00:33.666 --> 00:00:35.004
Nobody sent an email.

10
00:00:35.267 --> 00:00:40.500
Every few minutes, each clerk rang one colleague at random, and the two compared lists.

11
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:41.556
Map it.

12
00:00:41.556 --> 00:00:44.256
Each clerk is a node, one machine.

13
00:00:44.533 --> 00:00:50.428
The staff list is the node's map of the cluster: who's in it, and what each one says about itself.

14
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.223
Every node has two jobs.

15
00:00:52.223 --> 00:00:55.077
Hear each change, like a node that's leaving.

16
00:00:55.077 --> 00:00:58.123
And keep hearing that every other node is alive.

17
00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:04.283
For that, each node raises a counter once a second, its heartbeat, that just says still here.

18
00:01:04.667 --> 00:01:12.311
The obvious way: whoever changes tells everyone, and every node sends its heartbeat to every other node, once a second.

19
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:17.370
For six nodes, that's 6 times 5, 30 messages a second.

20
00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:17.811
Fine.

21
00:01:17.811 --> 00:01:19.401
For 30 nodes, 870.

22
00:01:19.401 --> 00:01:23.464
For a thousand, about a million, every second.

23
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:27.397
The machines would spend more time on the phone than at their desks.

24
00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:29.697
And there's a quieter problem.

25
00:01:29.697 --> 00:01:34.266
A node that was restarting when the news went out never hears it again.

26
00:01:34.266 --> 00:01:35.424
Nobody calls back.

27
00:01:35.867 --> 00:01:37.554
So do what the clerks did.

28
00:01:37.554 --> 00:01:44.499
Once a second, each node tells one random peer what it knows, and that peer passes it on in its own rounds.

29
00:01:44.733 --> 00:01:48.201
cass6 has news: it's leaving, version 412.

30
00:01:48.201 --> 00:01:51.091
How many rounds until all six know?

31
00:01:51.091 --> 00:01:52.412
Pause and guess.

32
00:01:55.633 --> 00:01:56.653
Round one.

33
00:01:56.653 --> 00:01:58.488
cass6 rings cass2.

34
00:01:58.488 --> 00:01:59.813
Now two know.

35
00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:00.990
Round two.

36
00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:06.685
Both of them ring someone: cass6 picks cass1, cass2 picks cass3.

37
00:02:06.685 --> 00:02:07.575
Four know.

38
00:02:07.575 --> 00:02:08.553
It doubled.

39
00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:09.654
Round three.

40
00:02:09.654 --> 00:02:15.345
This time cass5, who hasn't heard, rings cass1, and gets the news in the answer.

41
00:02:15.345 --> 00:02:18.191
News flows both ways, pushed and pulled.

42
00:02:18.191 --> 00:02:20.539
All six know, after three rounds.

43
00:02:20.767 --> 00:02:30.030
The number that knows roughly doubles every round, so it reaches N nodes in about log2 of N rounds: the number of doublings to get there.

44
00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:32.126
About ten for a thousand nodes.

45
00:02:32.126 --> 00:02:35.101
The lesson's demo counts 8 rounds for 6,000.

46
00:02:35.333 --> 00:02:39.538
And a round costs about three small messages per node, not a million.

47
00:02:39.538 --> 00:02:43.133
A node that missed the news just hears it at its next call.

48
00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:44.641
One more saving.

49
00:02:44.641 --> 00:02:49.523
Sending your whole map every second is a waste when almost nothing changed.

50
00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:57.250
So each node numbers every change to its own state: its heartbeat took 411, leaving took 412.

51
00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:59.413
That number is the version.

52
00:02:59.667 --> 00:03:05.647
Two nodes first swap short summaries, called digests: for each node, just its highest version.

53
00:03:05.867 --> 00:03:11.319
cass2's copy says 410, so it answers: send me what's newer than 410.

54
00:03:11.319 --> 00:03:13.965
And only those two states travel.

55
00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.884
Three messages: Syn, Ack, Ack2.

56
00:03:16.884 --> 00:03:18.096
That's gossip.

57
00:03:18.567 --> 00:03:20.340
Two details keep it honest.

58
00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:27.497
A restarted node starts counting from zero again, so its versions look older than what the cluster remembers.

59
00:03:27.767 --> 00:03:33.967
So each node also carries a generation, its start time, and a higher generation wins first.

60
00:03:33.967 --> 00:03:40.985
In the lab, cass3 came back with a bigger generation and heartbeat 32, and everyone took the new state.

61
00:03:41.233 --> 00:03:43.484
And a brand-new node knows nobody.

62
00:03:43.484 --> 00:03:47.391
So its settings list a few seeds, nodes to ask for the map.

63
00:03:47.667 --> 00:03:55.375
One rule: never list a new node as its own seed, or it skips copying its data and answers reads with empty rows.

64
00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:59.054
So nobody is in charge, and nobody needs to be.

65
00:03:59.054 --> 00:04:04.959
Each node rings one random peer a second, the news doubles, and only what's new travels.

66
00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:08.829
But gossip only carries what each node says about itself.

67
00:04:08.829 --> 00:04:10.866
A dead node says nothing at all.

68
00:04:10.866 --> 00:04:13.476
How the others read that silence is next.

69
00:04:13.967 --> 00:04:15.112
One quick check.

70
00:04:15.112 --> 00:04:18.692
A cluster of 1,000 nodes, and one of them changes.

71
00:04:18.692 --> 00:04:21.914
Roughly how many rounds until everyone knows?

72
00:04:21.914 --> 00:04:23.060
Pause and think.

73
00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:27.091
About ten.

74
00:04:27.091 --> 00:04:30.495
A thousand is about two, doubled ten times.
